// Deploy & Harden Phase 5 — Firestore direct-access security rules.
//
// This project's whole data-access model (CLAUDE.md's "Firestore is the
// only datastore" section) is: the Python backend (tools/firestore_client.py,
// using the server-side google-cloud-firestore Admin SDK under a service
// account) is the ONLY thing that ever reads or writes Firestore.
// Confirmed directly, not assumed: the frontend client bundle
// (frontend/client/src/) has zero references to the Firebase Firestore
// client SDK anywhere — every read/write goes through the authenticated,
// estate-scoped, role-checked REST API in frontend/api_v1.py.
//
// The Admin SDK always bypasses Security Rules — these rules exist
// PURELY to close off DIRECT client-SDK access (the case where someone
// obtains a valid Firebase ID token — trivial, since Firebase API keys
// are public by design, restricted by rules rather than secrecy — and
// tries to query Firestore straight from a browser, bypassing every
// estate-scoping/RBAC/policy-engine check that only exists in the
// Python API layer). Since the app never needs client-SDK access at
// all, the correct rule is unconditional denial, not a narrower
// per-collection allowlist that would still leave a client-reachable
// surface to audit.
//
// If a future feature genuinely needs client-SDK Firestore access
// (e.g. a real-time listener), it must be added here explicitly, scoped
// to exactly what it needs, with its own justification — not by
// loosening this default.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
